WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.590
Learning is durable change in capability.

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:14.243
A learner can explain more clearly, make a better decision, perform a step more accurately, recognize a cue earlier, or apply an idea in a new situation.

3
00:00:14.243 --> 00:00:18.127
Neuroplasticity helps explain why durable learning requires active engagement.

4
00:00:18.127 --> 00:00:27.191
The nervous system changes more reliably when learners use the skill, retrieve information, correct errors, and repeat the process over time.

5
00:00:27.191 --> 00:00:30.212
Memory formation is practical, not just academic.

6
00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:36.686
People need to encode information, revisit it, retrieve it, and connect it to existing knowledge.

7
00:00:36.686 --> 00:00:43.592
Practice and retrieval matter because they force the learner to bring information back and use it.

8
00:00:43.592 --> 00:00:51.361
Rereading may feel fluent, but recall and application provide stronger evidence that the learning is available when needed.

9
00:00:51.361 --> 00:00:57.835
Repetition with variation helps skill formation because real work rarely looks exactly like the lesson.

10
00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:04.309
Exact repetition can build familiarity, but variation teaches what changes and what stays the same.

11
00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:12.078
A learner who practices several versions of a task is more likely to recognize the underlying pattern later.

12
00:01:12.078 --> 00:01:16.825
Feedback and correction make those repetitions useful rather than simply familiar.

13
00:01:16.825 --> 00:01:24.163
Automaticity can develop when parts of a skill are practiced enough that they require less conscious effort.

14
00:01:24.163 --> 00:01:28.047
That can free attention for judgment, monitoring, and adaptation.

15
00:01:28.047 --> 00:01:30.205
But automaticity is not perfection.

16
00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:35.384
It needs safeguards, review, and continued feedback when the stakes are high.

17
00:01:35.384 --> 00:01:42.290
Neuroplasticity supports skill development, but it does not replace deliberate practice, good design, or responsible review.
